#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048823 :topic/difficulty 0.7171124966032248 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428406 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: linear=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1856->1861. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426} :encompassing/weight 0.5} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428407 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: linear=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1856->3029. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527} :encompassing/weight 0.5}] :topic/id #uuid "c321a6fc-a622-5c4d-89a5-d91936b9920c" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119627 :question/id #uuid "1c4e02bb-5ec0-572e-92ba-46d16c6f03fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-4819" :question/problem "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. If $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\n![](images/51/517c8b69f022d892daa4f8f48a74f6a93b12143e71f5077a046133b1b396ee9f.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A\\sim B,$ the equation $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the equation $B\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{4} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is $x_1=0$ and $x_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3 - \\dfrac{2}{3} x_4$ where $x_3$ and $x_4$ are free variables. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that\n\n![](images/50/50bf947d3beddfb5be8eee27c0d77bf57f3e93204a8554ffffa1e4cc41f35956.svg)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cba14b5-62f7-5fbe-beb1-9b1511305cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Null Space of a Matrix Given a Row Equivalent Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119628 :question/id #uuid "1cbcbca0-c8b5-55c9-af93-9b9c0ba59ffb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4820" :question/problem "Given that  ![](images/b4/b489811024ece2341b91e0b9819714a988cf31a87598601a8a1257c02129698c.svg) find a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Any vector in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](images/94/94af7adea734ab0f38b5f8521fcf575cbc263cc1700a3144f682706711604859.svg)\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is\n\n![](images/4a/4a8f749660a2c8b71cd9fe61c97180fd143a3c06c754e75a1dc51c14622f03a2.svg)\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e66c269-233b-57f8-9dae-46eb78c228bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for a Given Null Space"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119818 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119629 :question/id #uuid "a9d2ed82-2ba5-53a6-973d-c859ea5c295f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4821" :question/problem "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix ![](images/35/35651f90fba8141cafc7d513670e9903a4977a2c4bd3a8317ee7be90f4e7c493.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the following system:\n![](images/af/afdd7aba38057c3f4b226c63d103c83d3a76171ae8ae2a900db4c875a9c4e023.svg)\n\nHere, $x_1$ and $x_3$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd) and $x_2,x_4$ are free variables corresponding to the non-pivot columns. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2,x_4\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ This implies that\n\n![](images/4d/4d430991c440bc6e0afad527930566f4c839476f8f1c4f1ee91f2f726d0ece27.svg)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c70963f-589d-532b-9286-559f68693df7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for the Null Space of a Matrix Given in Reduced Row Echelon Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119630 :question/id #uuid "ef3be538-d974-5371-bd5b-a7e2ef3df827" :question/math-academy-id "e-4822" :question/problem "Given the matrix ![](images/ec/ec919391ecd75553fc12f04d1cb6832c7019677ff0954eaffded42fd2cdb8cc2.svg) find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the only solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So $\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},$ which means that the basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is the empty set, i.e., $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\,\\}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd5a675-c692-505e-900b-d7500fb7c653" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for the Null Space of a Matrix That Is Not in Reduced Row Echelon Form"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1856 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :topic/title "Finding a Basis of the Null Space of a Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049009 :topic/difficulty 0.7422880338435959 :topic/id #uuid "d31d3400-00a2-5388-a09e-ece03d04c735" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151943 :question/id #uuid "fc21ef44-25b7-5157-bbdb-c1284d1aa7ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-6254" :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](images/fb/fb428b64d8d1fc020f343aea11f50ad92158ad4dcbf611b5decb44bd79ac221c.svg) Find a basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we need reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/7a/7abb144e511574809625c59d5ed6b7658983fc646bd0e6d6327f9f12356b8f08.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151944 :question/id #uuid "302059c1-d807-54d2-badc-a4a19b2f9c05" :question/math-academy-id "e-6255" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/b2/b22e2ffa6f6d88665e120e8dad82961e72f8f4a99484e8f81de311a66ab25988.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_3=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/28/28f79983a195fa0152832aebd334cd8e1aaaa977dd9016347a3152403fd390c7.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 17\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](images/45/4588c7d7ae4fd6cbc749373d83ab2d81e6a228f0cac03d5b959f4177f3f4cdf9.svg)\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/fa/fa2ad666a6b80eac26bad12db322fa361578d94327e230d7c520c950add78923.svg)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 5 \\cdot 2=10.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151945 :question/id #uuid "d5bcbec9-0b72-5ba1-ad84-c770cf3a0c0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6272" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$,  $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/af/afbd6ce90f2192c311c38ced9c1d9512c48bbf9a5e06236415ef9fe70883f9be.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2ccba053acf8d050e305c3768c970ae635f4583163a867b6ef11f4e565ac1ae.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -12 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n![](images/6b/6b4306172b78c1e59bf66c8c08bf864aac2bcab65d4b1cc08ba4ddab0e5f1da3.svg)\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_4$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/b3/b3681d7f6c71d06ede32616ceec12ccd71da28373ee88eab451211af5a579212.svg)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 2 \\cdot (-3) = -6.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2623 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050578}] :topic/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}]}
